---
/**
 * The full-bleed quote band — a line from an attorney over a photograph.
 *
 * From the FAQ QUOTE band of `CJ - FAQ.dc.html` (markup 189-208).
 *
 * ⚠️ THE QUOTE IS THE ARTBOARD'S INVENTION, ATTRIBUTED TO A REAL NAMED PERSON,
 * ON A PAGE THAT IS LEGAL ADVERTISING. Nothing in the WordPress mirror contains
 * this sentence, and Richard Jaffe has not been recorded saying it. It is built
 * as drawn on the client's instruction (2026-09-08), and it goes on the list the
 * firm has to confirm or replace before launch — beside the homepage's "Our
 * goals" pull quote, which is the same problem and already open.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE ATTRIBUTION IS NOT A REFERENCE, and that is a deliberate exception.
 * House rule 8 says a quote attributed to a person is a `reference` to that
 * person so the name and role cannot drift from their bio. That rule is for
 * MODELLED content; this band is hardcoded page chrome. The moment it is
 * modelled it takes an `attorneyQuote`, and the role below stops being a string
 * that can go stale.
 *
 * ⚠️ THE QUOTE IS ONE COLOUR NOW, and the board's gold middle clause is gone.
 * `attorneyQuote` carried a second `accent` field for exactly that effect and it
 * was removed on the client's call — it costs an editor a decision about where a
 * sentence ends every time they write a quote, and the words belong to the quote
 * either way. Reusing the shared type inherits that decision, which is the point
 * of reusing it rather than inventing a second quote object.
 *
 * ⚠️ NOT THE BOARD'S `quote-bg-partners-walk.png`. That is the same shoot, the
 * same three men and the same tree-lined setting as the contact form's
 * photograph — which on the FAQs page sits a few hundred pixels below this band,
 * so the two read as one picture printed twice.
 *
 * This is the whole team on the street (2119x1054), chosen from seven candidates
 * rendered under this exact scrim (2026-09-08). What ruled the others out is
 * worth keeping, because the obvious picks are the wrong ones: `partners-park`
 * and `partners-sidewalk-team` are more tree-lined shots and repeat the very
 * problem being fixed; `attorneys-hero-team` is so wide (3.37:1) that a 420px
 * band crops it to a black strip; and `partners-harbor` is nearly square, so it
 * loses most of the frame to the crop. A band this shallow wants a ratio near
 * 2:1 and a subject spread across the frame rather than gathered in it.
 */
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import quoteBg from "../assets/quote-team-street.png";
import type { FAQS_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT } from "../sanity/sanity.types";

interface Props {
  quote: NonNullable<NonNullable<FAQS_PAGE_QUERY_RESULT>["quote"]>;
}

const { quote } = Astro.props;
const attorney = quote.attorney;

/**
 * ⚠️ THE PHOTOGRAPH IS NOT A FIELD. It is large decorative art with no editorial
 * decision in it, so it lives in `src/assets/` and goes through Astro's pipeline
 * — which fingerprints it, converts to WebP and builds the srcset at build time
 * (AGENTS.md rule 5). The frame is also chosen for this band's shallow ratio;
 * an editor swapping it for a squarer one would silently crop the group out.
 */
---

<section class="quote-band">
  <Image
    class="quote-band__image"
    src={quoteBg}
    alt=""
    widths={[900, 1400, 2119]}
    sizes="100vw"
    loading="lazy"
  />
  <div class="quote-band__scrim"></div>

  <div class="container quote-band__inner">
    <figure>
      <blockquote class="quote-band__quote">{quote.text}</blockquote>
      {
        attorney && (
          <figcaption class="quote-band__by">
            {[attorney.name, attorney.role].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}
          </figcaption>
        )
      }
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .quote-band {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    min-height: 420px;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The ground behind the photograph, for the moment before it decodes and for
       the strip either side of it on a very wide screen. */
    background: var(--forest-1000);
  }

  .quote-band__image {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* The three of them are walking toward camera in the upper half of the
       frame; anchored centre the band crops their heads on a short viewport. */
    object-position: center top;
  }

  /* ⚠️ A SIBLING, NOT A `::before` ON THE SECTION. Painting order inside a
     stacking context puts a negative-z pseudo-element BEHIND the element's own
     background but in front of nothing else — it would dim the forest ground and
     leave the photograph untouched. */
  .quote-band__scrim {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      rgba(21, 26, 17, 0.9) 0%,
      rgba(21, 26, 17, 0.82) 50%,
      rgba(21, 26, 17, 0.93) 100%
    );
  }

  /* Heavy on purpose, and heavier than a scrim over a photograph usually wants:
     42px italic serif has to hold against a busy frame. The photograph reads as
     texture here rather than as a picture of three people, which is the trade
     the board makes and the one the why-us band spent an afternoon proving is
     unavoidable — a wash light enough to show the picture cannot hold the copy. */

  .quote-band__inner {
    position: relative;
    text-align: center;
    padding-block: var(--space-xl);
  }

  /* The 420px floor and the full `--space-xl` assume a 42px quote on two lines.
     It wraps to four or five below tablet, so the floor is already exceeded by
     the copy and the padding is just dead height on top of it. */
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .quote-band {
      min-height: 0;
    }

    .quote-band__inner {
      /* ⚠️ HALF AGAIN ON THE STEP BELOW. Dropping straight to `--space-lg` /
         `--space-md` was right in direction and overshot: with the 420px floor
         gone, the step alone left the band sitting tight against the copy.
         `* 1.5` is the measured middle — 71px at tablet, 42px on a phone —
         rather than the next name up the scale, which is only about +25% and
         lands short again.

         ⚠️ THIS IS LARGER THAN THE DESKTOP PADDING AND THAT IS CORRECT. At
         1023px this resolves to 75px against `--space-xl`'s 62px at 1024 — the
         padding gets SMALLER as the screen grows, which is normally the ramp
         inversion this repo warns about. It is not visible here because
         `min-height: 420px` takes over at 1024, so the band still grows across
         the boundary: 312px to 420px. Capping it to `--space-xl` would undo the
         increase entirely, since the cap binds at every tablet width. If the
         420px floor is ever removed, this needs revisiting. */
      padding-block: calc(var(--space-lg) * 1.5);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .quote-band__inner {
      padding-block: calc(var(--space-md) * 1.5);
    }
  }

  .quote-band figure {
    max-width: 1040px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .quote-band__quote {
    font-family: var(--font-heading);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: var(--fw-regular);
    font-size: var(--fs-42);
    line-height: var(--lh-lead);
    color: var(--on-dark);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .quote-band__by {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-28);
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: var(--fs-14);
    font-weight: var(--fw-bold);
    /* The board's 0.22em — wider than `--ls-label`, and the widest tracking on
       the site. It is one short line under a 42px quote and needs the air. */
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    line-height: var(--lh-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--on-dark-62);
  }
</style>
